{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And the doors are shut.</b> They are the lips. Its parallel: \"the doors of his face, who opened?\" (Job 41:6)." "<b>When the sound of the mill is low.</b> It is the upper intestine, and it is the mesesser, which grinds the food. The word בשפל is a noun and an adjective, like \"טוב שפל רוח\" (Proverbs 29:23), its meaning being lowliness. The matter of בשוק is by way of allegory: when the sound of the mill is low, no flour is produced, and the shops of the bakers in the market will be shut. And דלתי and דלתות are found like שפתי and שפתות." "<b>And he rises at the sound of the bird.</b> It is known that food produces sleep, and when the mouth is shut, eating is cut off, and one wakes at a light thing." "<b>And all daughters of song are bowed down.</b> It is the throat, which formerly had a singer in it — its sound is bowed down so that it is not heard. And the word ישחו, which is masculine, comes with בנות, which are feminine, like \"וכל הנשים יתנו\" (Exodus 15:2). It is possible that both of them revert to the word כל."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {1 "Reading אננו as a scribal/typographical variant for הואנו (= הוּא נָנוּ?), i.e., a pronominal form serving adjectivally; the text here is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:29:19.987519953Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}